{
  "id": "ja-nein-fragen",
  "name": "Yes/no questions and short answers",
  "level": "A1",
  "subtitle": "A yes/no question starts with a helper verb: Do, Does, Is, Are or Can. Answer with a short answer: Yes, I do. No, she isn't. Don't repeat the main verb.",
  "briefMarks": ["Do, Does, Is, Are or Can","Yes, I do.","No, she isn't."],
  "source": "EnglishAcademy original English text (grammar-a1 run, 2026-10-29)",
  "concept": {
    "flow": [
      {
        "text": "Do / Does / Is / Are / Can",
        "variant": "verb"
      },
      {
        "text": "subject",
        "variant": "prep"
      },
      {
        "text": "main verb + rest?"
      }
    ],
    "exampleHtml": "Statement: She <span class=\"vp-prep\">works</span> here. → Question: <span class=\"vp-verb\">Does</span> she work here?",
    "qa": [
      {
        "html": "With most verbs, start with <strong>Do</strong> (I, you, we, they) or <strong>Does</strong> (he, she, it) + subject + the <strong>base verb</strong>.<br><em>Do you like tea? Does she work here?</em><br>✗ <em>Does she works?</em> → ✓ <em>Does she work?</em>",
        "tag": "Do / Does"
      },
      {
        "html": "With <strong>be</strong> and <strong>can</strong>, there is no do. Put the verb first.<br><em>He is a teacher. → Is he a teacher?</em><br><em>They are late. → Are they late?</em><br><em>You can swim. → Can you swim?</em><br>✗ <em>Do you can swim?</em> → ✓ <em>Can you swim?</em>",
        "tag": "be, can"
      },
      {
        "html": "A short answer uses <strong>Yes</strong> or <strong>No</strong> + subject + the <strong>same helper verb</strong>. Do not repeat the main verb.<br><em>Do you like tea? — Yes, I do. / No, I don't.</em><br><em>Is he tired? — Yes, he is. / No, he isn't.</em><br>✗ <em>Yes, I like.</em> → ✓ <em>Yes, I do.</em>",
        "tag": "Short answers"
      },
      {
        "html": "A negative question asks about something you may not expect: <em>Don't you like tea?</em> In English, the answer follows the <strong>fact</strong>, not the question.<br><em>Don't you like tea? — Yes, I do.</em> (I like tea.)<br><em>Don't you like tea? — No, I don't.</em> (I don't like tea.)",
        "tag": "Negative questions"
      }
    ],
    "noteHtml": "<strong>Yes</strong> always goes with a positive fact and <strong>No</strong> with a negative one: <em>Yes, I do</em> means I like it; <em>No, I don't</em> means I don't. You can also add a little more: <em>Yes, I do. I love it.</em> Next: <a href=\"/w-fragen\">Wh- questions</a>.",
    "reference": {
      "showLabel": "Show overview ▼",
      "hideLabel": "Hide overview ▲",
